# Decisions Log

Append-only, chronological record of non-obvious project decisions: what
was decided, why, and what alternatives were considered. README.md is
the current-state summary; this file is the trail of how we got there.
Every new non-trivial decision gets an entry here, appended at the
bottom, at the time it's made.

Format:

## YYYY-MM-DD — Title
Context: what prompted the decision.
Decision: what was decided.
Why: the reasoning.
Alternatives considered: (optional) what else was on the table and why
it lost.

---

## 2026-08-10 — v1 scope: Ashby scraping + TUI curation only
Context: README's original vision includes multi-board scraping,
scheduled refresh, AI-generated resumes/cover letters, and autonomous
agents.
Decision: this phase covers only Ashby ingestion, storage, fetch-time
filtering, and a TUI to browse/curate what's stored. Everything else is
explicitly deferred, not designed against yet.
Why: keep the data layer and curation workflow solid before building
anything that depends on it (resume generation needs good data first).

## 2026-08-10 — Company config lives in the database, not YAML
Context: README originally specified "configuration via yaml for
interesting companies."
Decision: companies and their filters are stored in the database,
added/edited via the TUI.
Why: user explicitly redirected away from YAML during the PRD
interview toward DB-backed config managed in-app.

## 2026-08-10 — Filter semantics: multi-value OR within a field, AND across fields
Context: needed to decide how multiple filter values combine.
Decision: multiple company_filters rows for the same field (e.g.
department) are OR'd together; distinct fields are AND'd together. A
field with no configured rows imposes no constraint.
Why: matches how a user naturally thinks about "Engineering OR Data
Engineering, AND Remote OR Canada" -- confirmed directly with the user
via concrete examples.

## 2026-08-10 — Filters gate ingestion, not storage queries
Context: whether filtering should happen as a SQL WHERE clause over
stored postings or in application code before storage.
Decision: filter.Match runs in Go, in memory, on postings fetched from
Ashby, before any store write. Non-matching postings are never
inserted.
Why: there's no row to filter yet at decision time -- it's an ingest
gate, not a query. Also keeps the matching logic pure/dependency-free
(microsecond tests, no DB fixture) versus dynamic per-company SQL
generation, which would need runtime WHERE-clause construction per
company's arbitrary filter set.

## 2026-08-10 — Changing a company's filters triggers a full re-fetch
Context: what should happen to already-fetched data when filters
change.
Decision: editing a company's filters triggers a fresh fetch against
current filters.
Why: user's explicit call; keeps filter changes from silently going
stale until the next scheduled/manual refresh.

## 2026-08-10 — Refresh is manual only in v1, no scheduler/daemon
Context: README's long-term vision includes scheduled refresh.
Decision: v1 only supports manual refresh (CLI `swamp fetch`, later
also a TUI keybinding). No background scheduling.
Why: smaller surface area for the first working version; scheduling is
additive later without reshaping anything already built.

## 2026-08-10 — Posting changes are tracked with history, never deleted
Context: how to handle a posting disappearing from a company's board,
or its content changing between fetches.
Decision: postings get a listing_status (open/closed) instead of being
deleted, and content/status changes are recorded as posting_history
snapshots.
Why: preserves markup/notes tied to a posting even after it closes, and
gives a real change trail instead of silently overwriting data.

## 2026-08-10 — Company lifecycle: soft pause only, no hard delete
Context: whether companies should support deletion.
Decision: companies can be paused (stop fetching) but never hard
deleted at the application level.
Why: user's explicit call -- avoids losing postings/markup tied to a
company by accident.

## 2026-08-10 — sqlc + database/sql over an ORM (GORM)
Context: user's prior experience was Rails/ActiveRecord, raised
"why not just use an ORM" directly.
Decision: hand-written SQL + sqlc-generated typed Go, not GORM.
Why: Go's ORM ecosystem doesn't have ActiveRecord's maturity -- GORM
leans on reflection/hidden magic in a language culturally averse to
that (silent N+1s via associations, surprising zero-value handling).
sqlc keeps SQL visible and testable against a real sqlite file, no
runtime query-building layer to debug. Schema here is small enough that
an ORM's abstraction isn't buying much.
Alternatives considered: GORM (more ActiveRecord-like ergonomics, but
less idiomatic and riskier as schema grows).

## 2026-08-10 — goose over golang-migrate for migrations
Context: needed a migration tool to pair with sqlc (which doesn't
handle migrations itself).
Decision: goose, with migration files embedded via go:embed.
Why: simpler/less config than golang-migrate for a solo sqlite-backed
tool, and embeds cleanly into the binary.

## 2026-08-10 — Bubble Tea (+ Bubbles/Lipgloss) for the TUI
Context: needed a TUI framework.
Decision: Bubble Tea, using Bubbles for components (textinput, viewport)
and Lipgloss for styling.
Why: de facto standard for Go TUIs, Elm-architecture model is naturally
testable (Update is a pure function over messages), large component
ecosystem.

## 2026-08-10 — go.mod: minimum-version directive only, no explicit toolchain pin
Context: initially added an explicit `toolchain go1.26.1` line to
go.mod to pin the Go version. `go get`/`go mod tidy` kept silently
stripping it, and a Taskfile task was added to re-pin it after every
tidy.
Decision: removed the re-pinning entirely; go.mod carries only the `go
1.26.1` minimum-version directive, letting Go's own tooling manage the
rest.
Why: user called this out as fighting the tool -- go mod tidy dropping
a redundant toolchain line is intentional design (Go 1.21+ auto-selects
a compatible toolchain from the minimum version), not a bug to patch
around.
Alternatives considered: keep re-pinning via a wrapper Taskfile task
(rejected -- actively worked against the toolchain's own design).

## 2026-08-10 — No ON DELETE CASCADE except tags -> posting_tags
Context: default instinct was to add ON DELETE CASCADE on FKs.
Decision: every FK defaults to SQLite's NO ACTION (delete fails loudly
if dependents exist) except posting_tags.tag_id -> tags, which cascades.
Why: companies/postings are never hard-deleted by the app, so cascades
would only ever fire by accident and could silently wipe owned data
(notes, tags, interview history). The one exception is justified:
deleting a tag you created should remove its associations -- that's an
explicit, expected user action, not a surprise side effect.
Alternatives considered: cascade everywhere (the SQL default reflex;
rejected as exactly the kind of silent-data-loss risk this project
wanted to avoid).

## 2026-08-10 — Ingested vs. owned tables never share columns
Context: risk of naming collisions/confusion between data pulled from a
job board and data the user enters (e.g. a "status" field meaning two
different things).
Decision: postings/posting_history are ingested-only (never written by
the user); posting_markup/tags/posting_tags/interview_stages are
owned-only (never touched by sync). No table mixes both.
Why: user's explicit call for a clean architectural separation, so
there's no ambiguity about which subsystem owns a given column.

## 2026-08-10 — Source-agnostic companies/postings (source + source_ref/source_id)
Context: even though only Ashby is implemented, more job boards are a
stated future goal.
Decision: companies and postings key off (source, source_ref) /
(source, source_id) rather than Ashby-specific fields; postings.
raw_payload preserves the full source response for fields not yet
promoted to canonical columns.
Why: adding a second board later means a new adapter + new rows, not a
schema change. raw_payload means no data is lost even before a field
is canonicalized.

## 2026-08-10 — Soft-delete via deleted_at, not an active boolean
Context: companies originally had an `active` boolean for pause/resume.
Decision: replaced with `deleted_at` (null = active); tags also gained
`deleted_at` instead of supporting hard delete.
Why: on companies, "paused" and "deleted-by-mistake" are different
intents that shouldn't be conflated in one boolean. On tags, soft-delete
means deleting a tag doesn't cascade-destroy historical posting_tags
associations -- a tag's `ON DELETE CASCADE` becomes a fallback the app
shouldn't normally trigger, since the app always soft-deletes.

## 2026-08-10 — created_at/updated_at/deleted_at as a standard convention
Context: these are operational/audit metadata, not business fields.
Decision: applied consistently across owned tables (fixed two gaps:
tags and posting_markup were initially missing full created_at/
updated_at).
Why: consistency, and these fields exist to answer "when did this
happen," not to carry domain meaning.

## 2026-08-10 — gofmt: auto-format pre-commit hook, not just a check
Context: wanted formatting enforced automatically.
Decision: `.githooks/pre-commit` (version-controlled, activated via
`task hooks:install` since `core.hooksPath` can't itself be version
controlled -- .git/config is local, untracked, per-clone state by
git's own design) gofmt's staged .go files and re-stages them before
each commit.
Why: unformatted code should never be possible to commit, not just
flagged after the fact.

## 2026-08-10 — golangci-lint: explicit "standard" linter set, findings fixed not suppressed
Context: first lint run found 3 errcheck findings (unchecked Close()/
Write() errors on defer/cleanup paths) -- a commonly-debated pattern
some teams exclude.
Decision: pinned `linters.default: standard` explicitly in .golangci.yml
(rather than relying on golangci-lint's implicit default, which can
shift between versions) and fixed all 3 findings by explicitly
discarding/asserting on the errors, rather than adding an exclude rule.
Why: user's explicit call to keep the strict default rather than carve
out an exception for a pattern that's rarely actionable but still worth
the discipline.

## 2026-08-11 — Ashby client: verified live API before writing fixtures
Context: had a prior model of the response shape from documentation
summaries only.
Decision: fetched the real public endpoint before writing any decode
code, which corrected two assumptions (field is `descriptionPlain` not
`descriptionText`; there's a top-level `apiVersion`). Confirmed the
endpoint returns all postings in one call, no pagination.
Why: don't build against a remembered/summarized API shape when the
real one is one curl away.

## 2026-08-11 — Ashby decode structs: forgiving types for API evolution
Context: how much resilience to build against future Ashby API changes.
Decision: string types (not enums) for fields like employmentType/
workplaceType, raw JSON preserved via json.RawMessage (byte-exact, not
re-marshaled) per posting. Deliberately did *not* build a pluggable
multi-version API strategy.
Why: additive API changes cost nothing with Go's default
unknown-field-tolerant JSON decoding. A full version-switching
abstraction would be premature -- exactly one API version exists to
design it against.

## 2026-08-11 — filter.Posting is its own minimal type, not ashby.Posting
Context: whether the filter package should depend on ashby's posting
shape.
Decision: filter defines a small local Posting{Department, Location}
type; callers convert at the call site.
Why: keeps filter dependency-free and decoupled from any one board's
client shape (ashby.Posting carries many irrelevant fields --
descriptions, URLs, raw payload). store.Company/postings may become the
real match target once sync exists, so binding to ashby's shape now
would need unwinding later.

## 2026-08-11 — filter.Match: case-insensitive exact match; unsupported field errors
Context: two behavioral choices without an obvious default.
Decision: value comparison is case-insensitive but still exact (not
substring/fuzzy). An unrecognized filter Field returns an error rather
than being silently ignored or treated as never-matching.
Why: job board data casing is inconsistent in practice, so
case-sensitivity would silently miss real matches. On the error path:
filter has no dependency on store's company_filters CHECK constraint,
so it can't assume filters were validated upstream -- fail loud instead
of masking a caller bug (typo'd field, schema/code drift).

## 2026-08-11 — store.UpsertPosting creates the markup row transactionally
Context: every posting should always have exactly one posting_markup
row (default status 'new').
Decision: UpsertPosting wraps the existence check + create/update +
(on insert only) markup creation in one transaction via
Queries.WithTx, rather than requiring callers to remember a second
call.
Why: deep-module preference -- hide the plumbing behind a simple
interface rather than leaking a two-step protocol callers can forget.

## 2026-08-11 — UpsertPosting never touches listing_status
Context: whether re-ingesting a posting's content should implicitly
affect its open/closed state.
Decision: listing_status changes only via explicit
MarkPostingClosed/MarkPostingReopened calls, never as a side effect of
UpsertPosting.
Why: open/closed is a domain transition sync decides deliberately
(based on presence in a fetch), not an incidental side effect of
content refresh.

## 2026-08-11 — Tags soft-delete; list queries differ by purpose
Context: tag deletion needs to not destroy historical tagging data.
Decision: ListTagsForPosting includes soft-deleted tags (a posting's
tag history shouldn't vanish when a tag is retired); ListTags excludes
them (it's the pick-list for applying new tags).
Why: follows directly from why tags soft-delete in the first place --
the two query use cases genuinely want different visibility.

## 2026-08-11 — sync.PostingFetcher: sync's own interface, not ashby's
Context: wanted sync decoupled from any one job board client for
testability and future multi-board support.
Decision: sync defines its own minimal PostingFetcher interface;
*ashby.Client satisfies it structurally.
Why: lets tests use a fake instead of real HTTP. Later found via
self-review that the interface still returns []ashby.Posting
concretely, so it doesn't yet provide true multi-source pluggability --
corrected the doc comment to say so rather than build a speculative
fix with only one real implementation to design against.

## 2026-08-11 — Filter re-match: non-matching postings are left untouched, not kept fresh
Context: if a previously-stored posting stops matching a company's
current filters, should sync still keep its content up to date?
Decision: no -- filters gate whether a posting is touched *at all* on
each fetch, including updates to already-tracked postings. A posting
that stops matching just goes stale (though it's still closed normally
if it disappears from the board, since that check is filter-independent).
Why: literal reading of the README's "non-matching postings are never
stored" -- confirmed directly with the user rather than assumed.
Alternatives considered: keep tracked postings' content fresh
regardless of current filter match (rejected -- inconsistent with the
literal filter contract).

## 2026-08-11 — SyncAll continues past a single company's failure
Context: one company's fetch failing shouldn't block refreshing others.
Decision: SyncAll processes every active company regardless of earlier
failures; each Result carries its own Err.
Why: user's explicit call, and matches how a personal tool should
degrade -- one bad slug or network blip shouldn't stall the whole
batch.

## 2026-08-11 — SyncAll: sequential, not a goroutine fan-out/fan-in pipeline
Context: whether refreshing multiple companies should fetch
concurrently.
Decision: sequential per company.
Why: sqlite writes serialize regardless of fetch concurrency (single
writer without WAL/busy-timeout tuning), so a concurrent pipeline would
mostly just overlap network calls -- real complexity (channels, error
aggregation, non-deterministic test ordering) for a benefit that's
disproportionate at this data scale (a handful of companies). Revisited
directly with the user (who proposed fan-out/collect) and both agreed
sequential is right for now; easy retrofit later since SyncCompany is
already a clean per-company unit.

## 2026-08-11 — Content-change detection: full field comparison, history only on real diff
Context: how to decide when a re-fetched posting counts as "changed."
Decision: compare every ingested field between existing and incoming;
only write a content_updated history snapshot and call UpsertPosting
if something actually differs. An unchanged re-fetch touches the
database not at all.
Why: avoids updated_at churn and pointless writes on a no-op sync,
verified directly (a second identical sync produces an all-zero
Result).

## 2026-08-11 — Posting closure is filter-independent
Context: how to decide when a previously-open posting should be marked
closed.
Decision: any previously-open posting not present in the current
fetch's full (unfiltered) result set gets closed, regardless of
whether it would currently match the company's filters.
Why: a posting disappearing from the board is unconditional fact,
independent of whether this app chose to track it.

## 2026-08-11 — TUI: company management is the first increment, not the full README scope
Context: store initially only supported companies; postings/markup
came later.
Decision: scoped the first TUI increment to company list/add/pause,
deferred posting browsing until store supported it.
Why: matched what was actually buildable at the time rather than
building UI against not-yet-existing data access.

## 2026-08-11 — TUI Update stays side-effect-free; I/O wrapped in tea.Cmd
Context: Bubble Tea's Elm architecture convention.
Decision: every store/sync call happens inside a tea.Cmd, producing a
typed message that Update handles on a later call -- never a direct
call inside Update itself.
Why: this is what makes Update testable without a real terminal: tests
call Update directly, manually execute any returned Cmd, and feed the
result back in.

## 2026-08-11 — TUI list scrolling: custom visibleWindow(), not bubbles/viewport
Context: company/posting lists didn't scroll -- the cursor could move
off-screen with no way to see it.
Decision: a pure visibleWindow(cursor, n, rows) function (centers the
cursor, clamped at the edges) used by both list screens, not
bubbles/viewport.
Why: these are rows of discrete items with their own cursor logic
already built -- viewport is designed for one block of pre-rendered
text, and layering it under an existing cursor system would mean two
overlapping scroll mechanisms instead of one simpler purpose-built one.

## 2026-08-11 — TUI posting detail: bubbles/viewport, unlike the lists
Context: the description text had no scroll mechanism and ran off the
bottom of the terminal.
Decision: the detail body (fields + description) is a viewport.Model;
up/down scroll it. Left/right, previously up/down, now move to the
prev/next posting.
Why: this genuinely is one long scrollable text blob -- exactly
viewport's designed use case, unlike the lists. Freed up/down (the more
natural scroll keys) by moving posting-to-posting navigation to
left/right.

## 2026-08-11 — Browser-open: pure command-selection function, OS process is unverified
Context: needed to open a posting's URL cross-platform (open/xdg-open/
rundll32) without adding a dependency for one exec call.
Decision: split into browserCommand(goos, url) -- a pure function
returning which command/args to use, fully unit tested for all three
platforms plus the unsupported-OS error path -- wrapped by a thin
tea.Cmd that actually spawns the process (not unit tested, same
category as View() rendering).
Why: separates the genuinely testable branching logic from the
actually-untestable-without-a-real-OS side effect, rather than leaving
the whole thing unverified.

## 2026-08-11 — ListPostingsByCompany ordering: added id DESC tiebreaker
Context: user spot-checked posting details against the database and
found apparent mismatches; investigation found ORDER BY first_seen_at
DESC had no tiebreaker, and sqlite's CURRENT_TIMESTAMP has only
second resolution -- a single sync inserts ~130 rows in one loop, so
most share an identical first_seen_at.
Decision: added `id DESC` as a secondary sort key.
Why: verified concretely that ties exist in practice (inserted 3 rows
with identical timestamps, confirmed the query plan uses a temp
b-tree for the sort, not a naturally-ordered scan) -- the observed
order was stable across repeated test runs, but nothing in SQL
semantics guarantees that holds as data volume or the SQLite version
changes. The original mismatch report turned out to not be caused by
this (user later said the bug seemed resolved on its own), but the
missing tiebreaker was a real latent correctness gap worth fixing
regardless.

## 2026-08-12 — Posting detail long lines: word-wrap by default, no toggle, no horizontal scroll
Context: description/URL fields longer than the terminal width ran
off-screen; viewport has latent horizontal-pan support but it's not
wired up (left/right are already claimed for prev/next posting).
Decision: word-wrap content to the viewport's width before
SetContent, re-wrapping on resize. No wrap/raw toggle, no horizontal
scroll.
Why: description text is prose, not tabular/fixed-width content --
wrapping is simply correct for that and needs no new keybinding to
discover. A toggle would add a mode for a need (viewing raw
fixed-width content) that doesn't exist in this data. Horizontal
scroll is the right tool only for genuinely wide fixed-format content,
which doesn't apply here; noted as an easy option to add later
without conflicting with this if that ever changes.
Alternatives considered: wrap/raw toggle keybinding; horizontal
scroll via viewport's native pan (would require moving posting
navigation off left/right).

## 2026-08-12 — Decision history: decisions.log, backfilled from project start
Context: wanted traceable history of project decisions beyond what's
implicit in commit messages.
Decision: this file. Append-only, one entry per non-trivial decision,
going forward from now -- and, per explicit request, backfilled for
the major decisions already made this session rather than starting
empty.
Why: commit messages capture the "why" for what shipped, but don't
give a single browsable chronological trail across the whole project,
and can get lost/summarized away in a squash or rebase. This file is
the durable record independent of git history shape.

## 2026-08-12 — Word-wrap implementation: cellbuf.Wrap directly, not lipgloss.Style.Width()
Context: implementing the word-wrap decision above.
Decision: wrapToWidth uses github.com/charmbracelet/x/cellbuf's Wrap
function directly, not lipgloss.NewStyle().Width(w).Render(). Also:
re-wrap explicitly on every tea.WindowSizeMsg while viewing detail,
by re-calling showPostingDetail() (which re-wraps from the posting's
original content), rather than just updating detailViewport.Width.
Why: two things were verified empirically, not assumed. First,
lipgloss's Width() also pads short lines to fill the width (it's a
box-layout primitive, not pure text wrapping) -- confirmed by a
failing test expecting unchanged short content. Second,
viewport.View() renders via lipgloss MaxWidth, which *truncates*
content wider than the current Width rather than re-wrapping it --
so just resizing detailViewport.Width on a window resize would
silently cut off text instead of reflowing it. Confirmed with a test
that specifically checks a marker word survives a resize (a
weaker "no line exceeds width" check alone doesn't catch truncation,
since truncated lines also satisfy that property).
